## Additional verified evidence from task-c29380b3 instance
This task instance confirmed the **horizontal** case and clarified the 8-line placement:
- Demo A (8x15): background 0, divider row 6 (all 2), object 4 at rows 3-4, cols 0-2. The object is adjacent to the divider; output keeps the object unchanged and paints the entire row 3 (immediately beyond its far/top edge) with 8.
- Demo B (25x4): background 9, divider row 10 (all 2), object 3 at rows 20-22, cols 0-2. Output moves the object upward to rows 11-13, adjacent to the divider, clears its original cells, and paints row 14 with 8 (the line just beyond the relocated object's far/bottom edge).
- Solved test (9x20): background 5, divider row 7 (all 2), object 1 occupying rows 4-6, cols 0-6 with an irregular pattern, already immediately adjacent above divider. Correct output leaves all object cells unchanged and paints the full row 3 with 8. Evaluator confirmed correct. A first incorrect attempt painted row 4 instead: when an object cannot move because it is already adjacent to the divider, the 8-line is on the far side of the whole object, not on its nearest-to-divider row.

## Refined rule
The line of 8 is a full grid-spanning line parallel to divider, on the side of the object's far edge away from the divider. When object has room, move it one cell toward the divider, preserving shape; paint 8 on the line immediately beyond the object's **new** far edge (equivalently, former outermost edge if that is the vacated line). When the object is already adjacent to the divider, do not move it; place 8 on the next empty line just beyond its far edge. Preserve dimensions, divider, and all other cells. The orientation can be horizontal or vertical; examples establish either."
